const T = [
  ['c. 1800–1000 BCE', 'Omens for the king', 'Scribes in Mesopotamia write down what was seen in the sky and what followed on earth. The great collection, named Enuma Anu Enlil after its opening words, holds about seven thousand omens on some seventy tablets. They concern the king, the harvest and war: an eclipse in a certain month means danger to the king of a certain land. Ordinary people have no place in it yet.'],
  ['c. 650 BCE', 'Reports to the palace', 'Scholars at the Assyrian court in Nineveh send the king written reports of what they have observed and which omens apply. Hundreds of these letters survive. Astronomers in Babylon begin the diaries that will record the sky night by night for six hundred years, the longest run of scientific observation from the ancient world.'],
  ['5th century BCE', 'Twelve equal signs', 'Babylonian astronomers divide the band of sky the sun travels through into twelve equal parts of thirty degrees, each named for a constellation in it. This is the zodiac. It was made for calculation: it let them state a planet’s place as a number.'],
  ['410 BCE', 'The first birth horoscope', 'The oldest known horoscope is a cuneiform tablet giving the positions of the planets at the birth of a child in Babylonia. About thirty such tablets survive. The sky is now read for one person, and the idea behind every later horoscope is in place.'],
  ['c. 300–100 BCE', 'The Greek synthesis in Egypt', 'After Alexander’s conquests, Babylonian methods meet Greek geometry and Egyptian traditions in Egypt under the Ptolemies. The result is the system still used in the West: the sign rising on the eastern horizon at the moment of birth (in Greek the horoskopos, “hour-marker”), twelve houses counted from it, and aspects, the angles between planets. The Egyptian decans, thirty-six star groups used to tell the hours of the night, are folded in.'],
  ['c. 130 BCE', 'The sky is found to drift', 'The astronomer Hipparchus finds that the equinoxes slip slowly backward against the stars, about one degree in seventy-two years. A zodiac measured from the spring equinox and a zodiac fixed to the stars will part company. Nobody treats this as urgent, and the two are now about twenty-four degrees apart.'],
  ['1st–2nd century CE', 'Rome, and the textbooks', 'Astrology is fashionable and politically dangerous in Rome. Augustus publishes his own horoscope and puts his sign on coins, and in 11 CE forbids astrologers to predict anyone’s death. In Alexandria about 150 CE Claudius Ptolemy, the leading astronomer of antiquity, writes the Tetrabiblos, which argues that the planets act on people as physical causes, in the way the sun plainly does. Vettius Valens collects more than a hundred worked charts.'],
  ['2nd–6th century', 'India takes up the zodiac', 'India had its own sky calendar: the twenty-seven or twenty-eight nakshatras, the stations of the moon, known from the Vedas. Greek horoscopy arrives by sea trade with Roman Egypt. A Sanskrit work called the Yavanajataka, “The Horoscopy of the Greeks”, sets it out in verse. Indian astrologers combine the two, and in the sixth century Varahamihira writes the Brihat Jataka, still a standard text.'],
  ['c. 400', 'Augustine’s objection', 'Augustine of Hippo, who had consulted astrologers as a young man, argues against them. Twins are born under the same sky, he writes, and live different lives. Christian teaching also objects that fate in the stars leaves no room for free will. Astrology fades in the Latin West for several centuries.'],
  ['8th–11th century', 'Baghdad', 'The Abbasid caliphs have Greek, Persian and Indian works translated into Arabic. The moment for founding Baghdad, 30 July 762, is chosen by a group of astrologers. Abu Ma‘shar of Balkh writes the Great Introduction and a theory of history timed by the meetings of Jupiter and Saturn. In 1029 al-Biruni writes a full textbook of the art and says in it that he does not trust its conclusions. Philosophers including al-Farabi and Ibn Sina write against it.'],
  ['12th–15th century', 'Back into Europe', 'Translators in Spain turn the Arabic books into Latin. Astrology is taught in the universities alongside astronomy and medicine: a physician is expected to know which sign rules which part of the body and when to let blood. Kings and popes keep astrologers. Dante places the astrologer Guido Bonatti in hell among the fortune-tellers, with his head turned backward.'],
  ['1496', 'Pico’s attack', 'Giovanni Pico della Mirandola’s Disputations against Divinatory Astrology, published after his death, argues at length that astrologers disagree with one another and that their predictions fail. In 1586 Pope Sixtus V forbids predicting the future of individuals from the stars.'],
  ['1543–1687', 'The parting', 'Copernicus puts the sun at the centre. Kepler, who works out how the planets move, also casts horoscopes for a living and calls astrology the foolish daughter who supports her wise mother, astronomy. Galileo casts charts for his patrons. By the time of Newton’s Principia in 1687 the planets move by gravity, and astrology has left the universities. William Lilly’s Christian Astrology of 1647 is the last great English textbook of the old kind.'],
  ['18th century', 'The almanacs', 'Astrology survives in cheap printed almanacs, sold by the hundred thousand, which mix the calendar, the tides and the weather with predictions for the year. Old Moore’s Almanack, begun in 1697, is still printed.'],
  ['1781–1930', 'New planets', 'Uranus is found in 1781, Neptune in 1846 and Pluto in 1930. The seven-planet system that had stood since Babylon had no place for them. Astrologers gave each a meaning within a few decades of its discovery and assigned it to a sign.'],
  ['c. 1890–1917', 'Character instead of fate', 'Alan Leo, a London astrologer and Theosophist, sells short printed readings by post in the thousands. He simplifies the chart, gives most weight to the sign the sun was in, and describes character and tendencies where older astrologers had foretold events. Part of the reason is legal: he is prosecuted for fortune-telling in 1914 and again in 1917.'],
  ['1930', 'The newspaper column', 'On 24 August 1930 the Sunday Express in London prints an article by the astrologer R. H. Naylor on the horoscope of the newborn Princess Margaret. Readers ask for more, and Naylor is given a regular column. To write for every reader at once he sorts them by birthday, and within a few years the twelve short paragraphs, one for each sun sign, are the standard form. Before this, hardly anyone knew “their sign”.'],
  ['1960s–1970s', 'The age of Aquarius', 'Linda Goodman’s Sun Signs, published in 1968, is the first astrology book on the New York Times bestseller list. Astrology becomes part of the counterculture and of psychology of a Jungian kind: the chart as a map of the self. Computers begin to do in seconds the calculations that took an astrologer hours.'],
  ['1990s–today', 'Software and apps', 'Free chart calculators go online in the 1990s, and anyone can see their whole chart, where the column gave only the sun sign. From 2017 apps such as Co–Star and The Pattern send daily readings to phones, based on the user’s time and place of birth. Astrology now spreads as memes and shared screenshots as much as through columns.'],
];
