  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What changed, and what stayed</h2>
    <p>Three things changed. The subject moved from the state to the person: the first omens were about kings and harvests, and a horoscope today is about one reader’s week. The claim moved from events to character: a medieval astrologer said when a man would die, and a modern one describes what he is like. And the standing moved from science to pastime: Ptolemy and Kepler practised it as part of their profession, and no university science department teaches it now.</p>
    <p>The machinery stayed. The twelve signs, the seven classical planets, the houses and the aspects in a chart drawn today are the ones set down in Egypt about two thousand years ago, and the signs themselves are Babylonian.</p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Francesca Rochberg, <em>Babylonian Horoscopes</em> (American Philosophical Society, 1998) and <em>The Heavenly Writing</em> (Cambridge University Press, 2004); <a href="https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.06.29/" rel="noopener">reviewed in Bryn Mawr Classical Review</a>, which gives 410 BCE for the first known horoscopes.</li>
      <li>Ptolemy, <a href="https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Ptolemy/Tetrabiblos/home.html" rel="noopener"><em>Tetrabiblos</em></a>, translated by F. E. Robbins (Loeb Classical Library, 1940).</li>
      <li>Augustine, <a href="https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/120105.htm" rel="noopener"><em>City of God</em>, book 5</a>, chapters 1 to 7, on twins.</li>
      <li>David Pingree, <em>From Astral Omens to Astrology: From Babylon to Bikaner</em> (Istituto Italiano per l’Africa e l’Oriente, 1997), on the passage of horoscopy to India and Persia.</li>
      <li>Nicholas Campion, <em>A History of Western Astrology</em>, two volumes (Continuum, 2008–2009), on the medieval, early modern and modern periods, including Alan Leo and R. H. Naylor.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.britannica.com/topic/astrology" rel="noopener">“Astrology”, Encyclopaedia Britannica</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Dates before about 600 BCE are approximate. The date of the Yavanajataka is disputed among scholars, between the second and the fourth century or later.</p>
